WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.162 --> 00:00:01.120
SAAD ALI: Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:01.800
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.220
I'm a software
engineer at Google.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:04.240
And today I have Wes.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.300
He's a product manager
at Google with GKE.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.470
Wes, I understand you've been
working on the on-prem product

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:11.505
for Google.

00:00:11.505 --> 00:00:14.490
WESTIN HUCHINS: Yeah,
so last Cloud Next

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.830
was down in San
Francisco around July.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.034
And we unveiled
GKE On-Prem, which

00:00:19.034 --> 00:00:21.450
is a product that I've been
working on for quite some time

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:25.020
and trying to bring Kubernetes
or an opinionated Kubernetes

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.670
installation to an
on-prem environment.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.670
It's something we heard
from a lot of customers,

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.520
that they really wanted to
solve for hybrid scenarios

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.980
where they had workloads
that they couldn't quite

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.340
move to the Cloud.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.540
But they really wanted the power
of GKE, all of the management

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.700
features that come
with GKE, and they

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.350
wanted it-- to package
it up and put it

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.590
in their own environment.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.410
And so we've kind of
been working on that

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.030
and taking that idea forward.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.151
And that's sort of the
beginnings of GKE On-Prem.

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:52.650
SAAD ALI: That
sounds super awesome.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.190
So let's dig into
it a little bit.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.540
What does it actually look like?

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.445
How does it work?

00:00:56.445 --> 00:00:58.950
WESTIN HUCHINS:
Yeah, so GKE On-Prem,

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:03.060
the fundamentals for it are
actually based a lot on open

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:03.750
source--

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.270
both open source Kubernetes,
but a new project

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.834
that's spun up in the open
source called the Cluster API.

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:12.000
So this is something that
we've gotten a lot of help

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:12.930
from the community.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.570
Kris Nova at Heptio has
been one of the main drivers

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:16.830
behind the Cluster API.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.070
But we brainstormed this
idea a long time ago

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.370
around making
infrastructure declarative.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.480
And it was kind of a new idea
at the time for Kubernetes.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.300
We had like 40 or
50 installers and we

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.580
found they were all
doing their own thing.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.310
And we were like,
well, is there a way

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.050
that we can take all
the lessons we've

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.110
learned around abstracting
out infrastructure, making it

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.940
into a Kubernetes
compatible API,

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.150
and provide a nice
lightweight layer for us

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:48.080
to build a cluster spec?

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.330
What does a cluster look like?

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.150
How are the machine's
provisions--

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.600
this all was the genesis
of the Cluster API, which

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.850
is now forming the underpinnings
for how we build GKE On-Prem.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.940
So GKE On-Prem actually
uses a lot of the machinery

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.250
that we've used in the
open for Cluster API.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.610
We define what we want
the cluster to look like.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.530
We have a cloud
provider plug-in model.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.400
Right now, we're supporting
VMware using vSphere

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.770
as our underlying IaaS layer.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.220
But really one of the nice
features of the Cluster API

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.810
is its extensability.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.230
So while the scope
right now is on VMware,

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.690
there's opportunity for a lot
of the other cloud providers

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.810
to plug in and make
GKE On-Prem run

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.430
across these different
environments.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.320
SAAD ALI: And what
kind of capability

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.965
is the Cluster API and GKE
On-Prem going to enable?

00:02:36.965 --> 00:02:38.340
WESTIN HUCHINS:
It's really going

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.807
to enable people to treat
clusters more like cattle,

00:02:43.807 --> 00:02:45.390
is one of the big
underpinnings of it.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.960
It's like we've
been trying to say--

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.560
if you can define what the
shape and size of your cluster,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.620
check that into Git-Repo
or something like that,

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.140
you have sort of a
reproducible cluster.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.110
And if we can automate spinning
those things up and tearing

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.170
them down, and automate the idea
of adding nodes and removing

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.020
nodes, baking in some of
the node pull concepts

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.450
that we offer in GKE,
well, this allows

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.280
us to be much more
flexible in how

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.325
teams go in provision clusters.

00:03:10.325 --> 00:03:11.700
Quite frankly what
I've seen when

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.340
I talk to a lot of customers is
they have these clusters that

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.510
are really long lived.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.820
Someone's like, well, I
need to keep my cluster up

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.820
for two to three years
sometimes because I never

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:21.610
want to touch it.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.640
I just keep rolling upgrades.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.440
The problem is is
that at some point

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.980
you may hit features that do
require a cluster rebuild,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.800
and the Cluster API really makes
that process of standing up

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.230
a new cluster much easier.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.200
SAAD ALI: OK.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.900
So it sounds like Google
is using the Cluster

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.687
API to be able to provision
these on-prem clusters.

00:03:39.687 --> 00:03:41.520
It's going to enable
the power of everything

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.629
we have in Google Cloud
to show up in On-Prem.

00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:46.170
What are some of
the other components

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.430
that make up On-Prem?

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.305
WESTIN HUCHINS: Yeah.

00:03:48.305 --> 00:03:51.090
So the real power
of GKE On-Prem is

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.840
that it's an on-prem
Kubernetes product that

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.430
is managed by Google.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.750
So there's this connection
back to our cloud.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.650
We actually use a lot
of our Cloud Console

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.440
and our other container services
that run inside the Cloud

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.404
in order to make sure that
your cluster is healthy

00:04:07.404 --> 00:04:08.820
and also to make
sure that you can

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.040
utilize a lot of the
cloud-based tools

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.730
that we've built for containers,
things like CI/CD, our Cloud

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.490
Build product, any of
our security products

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.909
that work well with GKE, and
treat the GKE On-Prem cluster

00:04:20.909 --> 00:04:25.470
sort of as an
extension of a zone.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.720
It's just another cluster
that runs-- it just happens

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.015
to run inside your data center.

00:04:29.015 --> 00:04:31.230
SAAD ALI: And so if you have a
large on-premise data center,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:33.690
you could potentially use the
Cloud to burst workloads into?

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:34.980
WESTIN HUCHINS: Oh, absolutely.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.438
You'll start to
see us really start

00:04:36.438 --> 00:04:38.460
to invest more
around multi-cluster

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.364
tooling and making
these actions where

00:04:40.364 --> 00:04:42.780
it's like we can build tools
that migrate from one cluster

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:43.474
to another.

00:04:43.474 --> 00:04:45.640
You can choose to run certain
workloads in the Cloud

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.270
that take advantage of
things like GPUs and CPUs.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.970
Or you could keep things
local for data sovereignty,

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.740
regulatory compliance reasons.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.330
Those types of
features are really

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:55.770
what we want to light
up with GKE On-Prem.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.150
SAAD ALI: That super exciting.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.760
When can customers begin
to play around with this?

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.760
WESTIN HUCHINS: We
are actively driving

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:05.030
towards a mid-2019
release for GKE On-Prem.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:06.860
We are already
working with a number

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.095
of early design customers
and alpha customers

00:05:09.095 --> 00:05:11.220
getting this deployed inside
their lab environment.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.130
So, yeah, we're still iterating.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:15.340
We're still looking for
good feedback on it,

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.120
but we're driving
towards a 2019 release.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:17.750
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.340
And is that the
most exciting thing

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:20.825
that you're excited
about in the next year?

00:05:20.825 --> 00:05:22.610
WESTIN HUCHINS: I mean,
absolutely, right?

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:23.900
It's going to be a big--

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.610
this is honestly a massive
release for Google as a company

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.670
because it really starts to
sell the vision of where we're

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:33.590
going with true
hybrid environments

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:36.690
and getting customers an
onboarding path onto Google

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:37.190
Cloud.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.420
SAAD ALI: That sounds awesome.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.295
So switching gears a
little bit, what are you

00:05:40.295 --> 00:05:41.895
most excited about at KubeCon?

00:05:41.895 --> 00:05:43.940
WESTIN HUCHINS: I really
am interested in hearing

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:47.990
Bryan Green and Tim Hawkins
talk a lot about the early days

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.100
of Kubernetes.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:49.760
You know--

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:50.870
SAAD ALI: The war stories.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:53.660
WESTIN HUCHINS: I know I've
heard a lot from Joe and Craig,

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:58.580
and I'm interested to hear
how it got made inside Google.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.870
Being a Googler for
quite some time,

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.300
I know some of the
different hoops

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.810
you have to jump through, so I'm
really interested in checking

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:05.000
that out.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.380
I think it's later
today or something

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:07.920
like that, where they're
going to talk about that.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:08.150
SAAD ALI: Awesome.

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:09.450
I'm looking forward
to that, too.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:10.190
Thanks a lot, Wes.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:10.490
WESTIN HUCHINS: Awesome.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:10.989
Thank you.

00:06:10.989 --> 00:06:12.670
SAAD ALI: Take care.

